Drive / OneDrive import via Cloudflare Worker relay

Status: implemented. User uploads already go browser →
presigned B2 PUT → Go `Head` + finalize. Cloud import now reserves and writes
an outbox job; the Queue Worker downloads one bounded file and PUTs it through
a late-minted presign. The origin never holds raw import bytes. The parser VM is
the first application host that downloads parseable files after finalization.

This is “Go/Coolify never sees import payloads,” not “only the parser sees files.”
The Worker still sees bytes. Cloudflare already terminates API TLS.


Agreed points
-------------

- Feasible on Workers Paid. CPU (30s default, up to 5 min) is not the
  constraint: Drive/B2 `fetch()` wait does not count as CPU. HTTP wall-clock
  is unlimited while the caller stays connected. Free plan (10 ms CPU) is
  the wrong plan. Old Bundled/Unbound 10ms/30ms numbers do not apply.

- Keep current caps: 10 MiB free / 30 MiB Pro per file
  (`sourceupload.SourceMaxBytes`), 20 files per request
  (`MaxFilesPerUpload`), UI chunks of 3 at concurrency 3. One 30 MiB object
  fits a 128 MB isolate even if buffered.

- One file per Worker / Queue invocation. Do not put a 20-file wave in one
  isolate (runtime-update 30s grace is the only Cloudflare wall-clock risk,
  and sequential batches are the shape that hits it).

- Go fetches Drive/Graph metadata JSON (`size`, name, mime) and rejects
  oversize *before* enqueueing a Worker. That is not a file download. Do
  not download on Go to “learn the size.”

- Worker fully downloads one object into one bounded `Uint8Array`, then PUTs
  that fixed-length body to B2. Workers emits `Content-Length` for the typed
  array; manually setting the forbidden header does not work.

- Treat the relay as a job. Do not await the Worker on
  `POST /api/workspaces/{id}/sources/import`. The browser holds that request
  through the Cloudflare tunnel; a slow Drive transfer eats origin 524
  (~100s) and Coolify/Traefik idle limits. Today’s sync import already has
  this problem on a slow wave.

- Reuse the existing upload reservation / complete seam. The Worker is
  another PUT client, not a new ingest protocol. Do not give the isolate
  B2 app keys.

- Do not use the Postgres `ingest` job type for the relay. That job parses from
  the dedicated VM and assumes the blob is already in B2.

- `upload_sessions` remains the quota/object reservation. The separate
  `source_import_jobs` row is the transactional outbox, provider locator,
  attempt lease, retry state, terminal error, and polling record. A
  Cloudflare Queue consumer is the runner. `source_import_requests` stores the
  request fingerprint and complete 202 body so browser retries replay accepted
  and rejected results without calling the provider again.

- Enqueue an opaque `jobId` only. Provider locators remain in Postgres. Go
  obtains a fresh provider token at acquire time. The Worker downloads and
  measures first, then requests a fresh PUT presign immediately before upload.
  Each attempt gets a different incoming object key, so a stale presigned PUT
  cannot overwrite the bytes owned by a replacement lease.

- `waitUntil` after a 202 is not a job. It only lasts 30s after disconnect.

- After B2 `Head` succeeds, `FinalizeUploadSession` creates the file and
  enqueues ingest the way browser uploads already do. Frontend already
  waits for file terminal *after* the POST; it must not wait on the POST
  itself for the relay.

- Unknown-size Google exports reserve the smaller of the account cap and
  Drive's 10 MB export limit. An attempt-bound upload grant atomically settles
  `declared_size` to the measured bytes (gating any positive delta) before
  minting the PUT presign. Finalization keeps exact `Head` equality.


Pitfalls / risks
----------------

- Google access token in the Worker. The token can read more than one file, so
  every control request is timestamped HMAC, attempts have database leases,
  and authorization is never logged. Microsoft uses Graph's short-lived
  one-file `@microsoft.graph.downloadUrl`; its broad token stays in Go.

- Google-native Docs/Sheets/Slides have no usable `size`. Export-to-PDF
  is unknown until it finishes. Those jobs still start; the Worker aborts
  if the stream exceeds `maxBytes`. Keep that mid-download cap for binary
  files too (metadata can be stale or wrong).

- `completeSourceUpload` today rejects if `Head` size ≠ `DeclaredSize`.
  Import cannot keep that exact match for exports. Relax or update
  declared size from the uploaded object before finalize.

- Trust model. A relay still sees raw bytes. You are moving the trustee
  from the origin to Cloudflare, which you already use for the tunnel.

- At-least-once Queue delivery. Complete must stay idempotent (completed
  sessions already short-circuit). A retry after a successful PUT must
  not create a second file row. The browser sends one request id across HTTP
  retries, and expired database leases reopen the durable outbox.

- Queue delay vs Clerk token lifetime (~1h) and presign TTL (15 min).
  Why mint both at run, not enqueue. A long DLQ/retry still needs a
  fresh token fetch from Clerk via Go.

- Do not create a file row (with a raw URL) before the blob exists.
  `pending` today means “waiting for ingest,” not “still on Drive.”
  Overloading it 404s the viewer. Keep the in-flight state on
  `upload_sessions` (or add a distinct import status if the UI must
  show a row immediately).

- Inbound Worker request body is capped (~100 MB) and unused here — the
  job payload is small JSON. Do not send file bytes to the Worker.
  Do not buffer 20 files in one isolate.

- Caller timeout is still a cap *inside* the consumer: set a per-file
  budget (minutes), not a whole-batch budget.


Suggested implementation
------------------------

1. Metadata helper (Go)

   Extend the Drive/Graph metadata GET to ask for `size` (and keep
   name / mimeType). Reject binary files over `sourceMaxBytes` before
   any session or job is created. Google-apps types skip the size gate
   and carry `maxBytes` into the job. Leave `DownloadGoogleFile` /
   `DownloadMicrosoftFile` unused by import once the Worker exists;
   do not keep a Go download fallback that slurp-buffers.

2. Import POST becomes reserve + enqueue

   Same authz, workspace file room, and credits/ingest-slot checks as
   today. For each accepted ref: create an `upload_sessions` row
   (incoming key, declared size from metadata or a safe upper bound for
   exports) and a `source_import_jobs` row in one transaction. The outbox
   dispatcher sends only `jobId` and safely tolerates duplicate sends. Return
   `202` with job ids. Bind retries to a request id before reserving quota. No
   provider token or presign enters the message.

3. Worker / Queue consumer (one file)

   Authenticate the kick (HMAC or service binding). Ask Go for a
   short-lived attempt grant: fresh provider download authority plus the
   session’s maxBytes. Download (export Google-apps to PDF as
   today). Abort if bytes read exceed maxBytes. PUT to the presign
   acquired only after measuring the bytes. Do not store B2 app keys on the
   isolate.

4. Complete

   Worker (or Go, after a small “object is there” callback) runs the
   existing complete path: `Head`, promote incoming → final, finalize
   session. `FinalizeUploadSession` itself creates the file and optional ingest
   job; do not call `CreateSourceWithJob` / `CreateSourceReady` again. Failed
   and DLQ jobs release the reservation immediately, with the session sweeper
   as a backstop.

5. Frontend

   `drainImport` must stop treating the import POST as “bytes are in
   B2.” Poll sessions (or a thin import-job status) until each becomes
   a file, then keep `waitForFileTerminal` for ingest. Concurrency
   stays 3; that is enqueue concurrency, not download concurrency on
   one isolate.

6. Do not do

   Await Worker on the user request. Fire-and-forget HTTP +
   `waitUntil`. Put OAuth tokens or presigns in the queue. Reuse
   `jobs.type = 'ingest'` for the relay. Give the Worker B2
   credentials. Download on Go to measure size. Batch a whole wave
   into one invocation.


